<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Source: 29-CIsTesting-MeanDifference.Rmd -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-29-cis-testing-mean-difference" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Mean differences (paired data): CIs and tests</title>
    <idx><h>Research question</h><h>relational</h></idx>
    <idx>Mean difference</idx>
  <objectives xml:id="sec-analysis-paired">
    <introduction>
      <p>You have learnt to ask an RQ, design a study, classify and summarise the data, construct confidence intervals, and conduct hypothesis tests. <alert>In this chapter</alert>, you will learn to:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li><p>identify situations where mean differences are appropriate.</p></li>
      <li><p>construct confidence intervals for a mean difference.</p></li>
      <li><p>conduct hypothesis tests for the mean difference with paired data.</p></li>
      <li><p>determine whether the conditions for using these methods apply in a given situation.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <sidebyside width="65%">
    <image source="generated/SixSteps-ch29.png">
      <description>
        <p>A diagram showing the six steps of research, arranged in a cycle: Ask, Design, Collect, Classify and summarise, Analyse, Report. Step 5 (<em>Analyse</em>) is highlighted, captioned <q>CIs and tests: mean differences</q>.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>

  <section xml:id="sec-paired-intro">
    <title>Introduction: six-minute walk test</title>
    <p>
      The six-minute walk test (6MWT) measures how far subjects can walk in six minutes, and is used as a simple, low-cost evaluation of fitness and other health-related measures. The recommended setting for the test is usually a walkway of at least <m>30\ms</m>. <xref ref="saiphoklang2022comparison"/> measured the 6MWT distance when the same subjects used <em>both</em> <m>20\ms</m> and <m>30\ms</m> walkways.
    </p>
    <p>
      The comparison is <em>within</em> individuals (Sect. <xref ref="sec-rqs-repeated-measures"/>);<idx><h>Comparison</h><h>within individuals</h></idx> this is a <em>repeated-measures</em> study. Each subject has a <em>pair</em> of 6MWT measurements, and the study produced <em>paired data</em> (below),
    </p>
    <p>
      the topic of this chapter.<idx><h>Data</h><h>paired</h></idx><idx><h>Study types</h><h>paired</h></idx>
    </p>
    <table xml:id="tab-data-6mwt">
      <title>The six-minute walk test (6MWT) distance, for walkways of <m>20\ms</m> and <m>30\ms</m> length. These are the first five and the last five of the <m>50</m> total observations. (A negative difference means the <m>20\ms</m> distance is greater than the <m>30\ms</m> distance.)</title>
      <tabular halign="center" top="medium" bottom="medium">
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell><alert>Person</alert></cell>
          <cell><alert>20 m w'way</alert></cell>
          <cell><alert>30 m w'way</alert></cell>
          <cell><alert>Diff.</alert></cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>1</cell><cell>272.1</cell><cell>281.6</cell><cell>9.5</cell></row>
        <row><cell>2</cell><cell>425.3</cell><cell>454.4</cell><cell>29.0</cell></row>
        <row><cell>3</cell><cell>338.2</cell><cell>330.0</cell><cell><m>-</m>8.2</cell></row>
        <row><cell>4</cell><cell>240.0</cell><cell>271.0</cell><cell>31.0</cell></row>
        <row><cell>5</cell><cell>518.3</cell><cell>555.3</cell><cell>37.0</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\vdots</m></cell><cell><m>\vdots</m></cell><cell><m>\vdots</m></cell><cell><m>\vdots</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>46</cell><cell>245.2</cell><cell>298.4</cell><cell>53.2</cell></row>
        <row><cell>47</cell><cell>184.4</cell><cell>218.4</cell><cell>34.0</cell></row>
        <row><cell>48</cell><cell>400.0</cell><cell>413.9</cell><cell>13.9</cell></row>
        <row><cell>49</cell><cell>344.8</cell><cell>384.2</cell><cell>39.4</cell></row>
